WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.520
- [Instructor] So what we're
going to do in this video

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.840
is think about equivalent fractions.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:07.910
So let's say we have the fraction 3/4,

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:09.810
and I wanna think about

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:12.790
what is an equivalent number of eighths?

00:00:12.790 --> 00:00:17.360
So 3/4 is equal to how many eighths?

00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:19.750
And to represent that how many,

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:21.290
I could put a question mark there,

00:00:21.290 --> 00:00:24.230
but instead of a question
mark, I'll just put a letter.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:26.110
So what should y be?

00:00:26.110 --> 00:00:29.340
3/4 is equal to y/8.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.030
What does y need to be to make this true?

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:34.500
And before I tell you
go go pause this video

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.070
and try to work on it on your own,

00:00:36.070 --> 00:00:37.570
which I will do in a little bit,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.210
I'll give you a little bit of a hint.

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:43.390
So let's try to represent 3/4,
or I'll represent it for you.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:46.400
So I will do it with this rectangle.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:49.890
So I'm going to divide it
into four equal sections.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.510
So let's see, that would be
dividing it roughly in half.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.270
I'm hand-drawing it, so it's not perfect,

00:00:54.270 --> 00:00:55.640
but these should be equal sections.

00:00:55.640 --> 00:00:59.990
The areas of each of these
sections should be equal.

00:00:59.990 --> 00:01:02.840
So there you go, this is my
hand-drawn version of that.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.840
And so three of those four,
and I will do that in purple.

00:01:08.290 --> 00:01:11.003
Three of those four, it could be one,

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:17.290
two, and then just for kicks
I will do this one out here.

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:20.140
So that is 3/4 right over there.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:22.920
So if I were to think about
this in terms of eighths.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:25.840
So I'm going to draw another whole.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.100
But this time instead of just
splitting them into fourths,

00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:31.560
I'm going to split it into eighths.

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:33.830
So let's do the fourths first,

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:36.920
just 'cause it's easy to
look at the one above that.

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:38.430
So that's my fourths.

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:42.150
And then I'll divide each
of the fourths into two.

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:45.210
So that gives me eighths.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:47.160
All right, almost there.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.260
The drawing is really
the hardest part here.

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:52.500
And so each of these is an eighth.

00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:53.550
It's hand-drawn.

00:01:53.550 --> 00:01:56.160
But imagine if there were
eight equal sections.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:59.290
So how many eighths is equal to 3/4?

00:01:59.290 --> 00:02:01.840
Pause the video and try to
work it out on your own.

00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:06.410
All right, well, we can
just look at this visually.

00:02:06.410 --> 00:02:10.170
So this first fourth, we
could say that's equivalent

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.290
to filling out this
eighth and this eighth.

00:02:14.290 --> 00:02:17.380
So that first fourth is equal to 2/8.

00:02:17.380 --> 00:02:19.450
This second fourth is equal

00:02:19.450 --> 00:02:22.770
to another two of these eighths.

00:02:22.770 --> 00:02:25.210
And then this third fourth is equal

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:29.030
to another two of the eighths.

00:02:29.030 --> 00:02:31.450
So how many eighths have I shaded in?

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:36.260
Well, I have one, two,
three, four, five, 6/8.

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:39.120
So I have six over eight.

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:43.520
So 3/4 is equivalent to 6/8.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:47.430
So in this scenario, y is equal to 6/8,

00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:51.580
or we could say 6/8 is equal to y.

00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:54.723
Now let's do another example.

00:02:56.010 --> 00:02:58.890
So what we could see
here in this top circle

00:02:58.890 --> 00:03:02.360
is we've divided it
into six equal sections.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.870
So each of these are one of
the six equal sections or 1/6.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:09.560
And we can see that one, two, three, four

00:03:09.560 --> 00:03:11.100
of them are shaded in.

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:13.800
So what we have represented
in that top circle,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:18.253
that is four out of six.

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.770
So what I wanna think about is

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:25.040
how many thirds are equivalent to 4/6?

00:03:25.040 --> 00:03:26.560
Pause this video and think about it.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:30.690
So once again, how many
thirds are equivalent to 4/6?

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:32.120
Instead of just putting
a question mark there,

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.210
I'll put the letter x.

00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:36.400
So what should x be for these
two things to be equivalent?

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:38.100
Or another way to think about it is

00:03:38.100 --> 00:03:42.940
four over six is equal to

00:03:42.940 --> 00:03:47.240
x over three.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.823
4/6 is equal to how many thirds?

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.900
All right, now let's do this together.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.880
And so one way you could think about it,

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.990
let's see, for 1/3,

00:03:57.990 --> 00:04:01.110
for us to make this equal to 1/3,

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:04.940
it looks like that is equivalent to

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:08.660
what I am circling in the orange up here.

00:04:08.660 --> 00:04:10.230
And that also makes sense.

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:13.410
If I were to divided 1/3 into two,

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:16.460
so now I would have this would
be 1/6 and that would be 1/6.

00:04:16.460 --> 00:04:19.400
So you need 2/6 to make up 1/3,

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:22.660
or each 1/3 is equivalent to 2/6.

00:04:22.660 --> 00:04:26.900
So this is 1/3 right over here.

00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:30.940
And that is equivalent
to two of these sixths.

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.080
But we are not completely done yet.

00:04:33.080 --> 00:04:35.330
We have another two sixths.

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.140
So we could say those
two sixths are equivalent

00:04:37.140 --> 00:04:38.410
to another third.

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:40.760
And it's a little tricky
because they didn't put

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:42.750
this sixth next to that sixth,

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:44.750
but you could imagine if we were to move.

00:04:44.750 --> 00:04:46.480
Let's say we were to move this sixth.

00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:48.850
So I'm gonna color this one in white.

00:04:48.850 --> 00:04:52.963
So if I were to move that
sixth to right over here.

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:57.120
So we're shading this one in instead.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:02.120
So then you can see that these
two sixths right over here,

00:05:02.750 --> 00:05:06.720
these two sixths are equivalent

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.693
to this third right over there.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:15.060
So what you can see is, is that our 4/6,

00:05:15.060 --> 00:05:17.150
and remember, I moved this one over,

00:05:17.150 --> 00:05:20.030
so I'm not shading this one in anymore.

00:05:20.030 --> 00:05:21.540
But you can see the 4/6

00:05:21.540 --> 00:05:24.810
that I've shaded in is equivalent to 2/3.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.703
Or another way to say it
is x would be equal to two.

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:30.010
X would be equal to two.

00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:34.783
4/6 is equal to x/3,
or 4/6 is equal to 2/3.

